course: dictionary
lesson: 9386
title: Sprawl: spreading out
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sprawl is a word with two main jobs. A person or animal can sprawl, which means lying or sitting with arms and legs spread out. A town or city can sprawl too, which means it spreads out over a large area in an untidy way. Look at the words around sprawl to find out which one is meant.
explain: Read this: «Toy boxes and cartoons call every big old reptile a dinosaur, but scientists have a rule: a dinosaur stood with its legs straight under its body, not sprawled out to the side like a lizard's.» The words straight under its body are the clue. A lizard's legs stick out to the side, so sprawled out must mean spread out wide.
explain: Try the swap check. Put «spread out» in place of sprawled and read it again: «not spread out to the side like a lizard's.» It still makes sense, so you have found the meaning. You can use the same check for urban sprawl: «the city spread out over the countryside» fits well.
fact: To sprawl means to sit or lie with your arms and legs spread out in a relaxed or careless way.
fact: To sprawl can also mean to fall so that you land spread out, as in «sent someone sprawling» in football.
fact: A town or city that sprawls spreads out over a large area in an untidy way.
fact: Urban sprawl is the spreading of houses, shops and roads into the countryside around a city.
fact: Planners in the UK use green belts to control urban sprawl and protect the countryside.
exercise: «The puppy sprawled across the rug after its walk.» What does sprawled mean here? | Barked loudly at the postman | Sat up straight and still | Ran quickly around the garden | *Lay with legs and arms spread out, relaxed
exercise: «The town sprawled for miles along the river.» What does sprawled suggest about the town? | It moved quickly down the river | *It spread out over a large area, untidy | It was hidden under the hills | It was very small and neat
exercise: Find and copy the word from the dinosaur sentence that describes a lizard's legs spread out to the side. | stood | straight | *sprawled | body
test:
«He was tired, so he sprawled on the sofa with his arms out.» What does sprawled mean here? | *Lay relaxed with arms and legs spread out | Stood up quickly and stretched | Hid under a blanket | Fell asleep standing up
test:
«He tripped and sent the defender sprawling in the penalty box.» What does sprawling mean in this sentence? | Ran away quickly | Scored a goal | *Fell and landed spread out | Shouted at the referee
exam:
Urban sprawl means the spreading of houses, shops and roads into the countryside around a city. Why do planners in the UK try to control it? | *To protect the countryside with green belts | To spread houses further into the fields | To close all the shops in the city centre | To make the city bigger on purpose
